  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThe monsoon is now completely over us. Not everyone saw rain today, \nbut a lot of locations, especially West of the Divide, has some, and \na good amount in some locations. See still have some showers and \nembedded thunder tonight but the general trend for the night is \ndown. However, showers will continue for much of the night.\n\nThe main event with the monsoon still looks to be today. The \nheadliners in the monsoonal movie remain the same. The leading actor \nis obviously the monsoonal moisture. The guidance for the expected \nprecipitable water has dropped just a bit today, but it is still \nvery high. It now maxes out at around 1.50 inches, down from 1.61 \nyesterday. However, it has been running a bit hot by about 15 to 20 \npercent. So, in reality, the maximum, would be closer to 1.25 to \n1.35. However, this is still more than 200 percent of normal. The \nsupporting actor (or actress) is a shortwave moving in from the west \nthat will move into Wyoming this afternoon and evening. This will \nhelp give everything a little more lift of the synoptic variety and \nhelp enhance rainfall rates even more. We continue to have most of \nthe area under a slight risk of excessive rainfall. With this, we \nwill continue the flood watch as is for now. As for general timing, \nwestern Wyoming would see a vast majority of the activity through \nsunset. East of the Divide, it would be a later show, starting in \nthe afternoon and then continuing well into the evening before \ncoverage lessens after midnight. Watch for flooded roads, and always \nremember: Turn around, don't drown!\n\nThere will be one more day of concern across the area on Thursday. \nPrecipitable waters remain rather high on this day as well. However, \nthey do drop about 20 percent East of the Divide and around 30 \npercent West of the Divide. However, values are still between 150 \nand 200 percent of normal, so some flooding is still possible, \nalthough not as likely as today. The Excessive Rainfall Outlook has \nnot really changed, with a vast majority of the area still in a \nMarginal risk. One change is that the far southwestern portion of \nour area is now out of it. We still expect the majority of the \nactivity to be in areas East of the Divide. As for the flood \nwatch, we will leave it alone for now, but it may need to be \nextended, especially for areas East of the Divide. Most activity\nshould end shortly after sunset, but a few showers again may \nlinger after midnight. \n\nThe break finally comes in on Friday as some much drier air pushes \nacross most of the area. Precipitable water values finally fall \nunder one inch across much of the area, although still somewhat \nabove normal. The chance of convection is not zero on this day, \nalthough less than 1 out of 4 for all areas. However, it will come \nwith a side effect. One benefit of the deeper cloud cover is \ntemperatures have cooled somewhat. However, we still have ridging \nover us and with 700 millibar temperatures climbing back toward the \nplus 20 celsius mark, it will get rather hot again. Most locations \nbelow 6200 feet will again see highs into the 90s an d the warmer \nspots in the Bighorn Basin have at least a 1 in 2 chance of highs \nof 100 or greater.\n\nA bit more monsoonal moisture will move over the area for the \nweekend, but at this point values look more like 150 percent of \nnormal versus the 200 to 250 percent of normal we may see today. \nNevertheless, it will increase the chance of diurnal convection on \nboth days. And temperatures look to remain very warm as well, more \nthan likely similar to Friday, with widespread 90s in the lower \nelevations with triple digit highs in the warmer spots. Models are \nsplit for early next week though, with some showing drier conditions \nand others keeping the diurnal convection trend continuing. However, \nabove normal temperatures are likely to continue.",
